== Learning Objectives
<learning-objectives>
By the end of this module, you will be able to:

- Explain what CDO is and why it is essential for climate data
  processing
- Describe CDO's architecture and the operator concept
- Identify common climate data formats (NetCDF, GRIB, HDF5, GeoTIFF)
- Distinguish between different climate data sources (CMIP, reanalysis,
  weather, satellite)
- Navigate the course structure and understand what each module covers
- Install CDO and explore available operators

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)

== What is CDO?
<what-is-cdo>
Climate Data Operators (CDO) is a powerful command-line tool designed
specifically for processing and analyzing climate and weather data.
Developed and maintained by the Max Planck Institute for Meteorology,
CDO provides a comprehensive collection of operators that work with
climate data files in various formats.

#figure(image("../../assets/fig_global_temperature.png"),
  caption: [
    Real near-surface air temperature from the NCEP/DOE Reanalysis-2
    sample distributed with this course. Every chapter and notebook uses
    this and other real observational and reanalysis datasets.
  ]
)

`{note} CDO is free and open-source software. It handles the complexities of climate data formats so that researchers can focus on science rather than data wrangling.`

CDO operates on the principle of #strong[operators] - individual
commands that perform specific operations on climate data. These
operators can be chained together to create complex processing
pipelines.

== Why CDO is Important
<why-cdo-is-important>
Climate data processing presents several unique challenges that CDO
addresses:

`{important} **Efficiency**: CDO is optimized for large-scale climate datasets, often processing terabytes of data efficiently without requiring a high-memory environment.`

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (40.91%, 59.09%),
    align: (auto,auto,),
    table.header([Benefit], [Description],),
    table.hline(),
    [#strong[Efficiency];], [Written in C, CDO processes data much
    faster than equivalent Python or R scripts],
    [#strong[Standardized Operations];], [Hundreds of predefined
    operators for common climate data tasks],
    [#strong[Large Dataset Handling];], [Memory-efficient processing
    that can handle files much larger than available RAM],
    [#strong[Format Agnostic];], [Works seamlessly across NetCDF, GRIB,
    HDF5, and other formats],
    [#strong[Reproducibility];], [Command-line operations are inherently
    reproducible and scriptable],
    [#strong[Parallel Processing];], [Support for multi-threaded and
    distributed computing],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

== CDO Architecture
<cdo-architecture>
CDO's architecture is based on a #strong[stream-oriented data flow
model];:

```
Input File(s) → [CDO Operator(s)] → Output File
```

=== The Operator Concept
<the-operator-concept>
Each CDO command follows this syntax:

```bash
cdo [global_options] operator [operator_options] input_file output_file
```

Operators are organized into categories:

- #strong[File operations];: copy, merge, split, select
- #strong[Statistical operators];: mean, sum, max, min, std
- #strong[Temporal operators];: timmean, timsum, timmax
- #strong[Spatial operators];: fldmean, fldsum, fldmax
- #strong[Arithmetic operators];: add, sub, mul, div
- #strong[Grid operators];: remap, grid interpolation
- #strong[Conditional operators];: select, ifthen, mask

`{tip} Think of CDO operators as a toolbox. Each operator does one thing well, and you can combine them like building blocks.`

=== Data Flow
<data-flow>
CDO processes data as a #strong[stream];:

+ Read input file(s) in chunks
+ Apply the operator to each chunk
+ Write the processed result to output

This streaming architecture means CDO never needs to load an entire
dataset into memory at once.

== Climate Data Formats Overview
<climate-data-formats-overview>
Climate data comes in several formats, each with its own strengths:

=== NetCDF (Network Common Data Form)
<netcdf-network-common-data-form>
NetCDF (.nc) is the most widely used format in climate research.

`{note} NetCDF is self-describing - the file contains metadata about its contents, including variable names, units, dimensions, and coordinate information.`

#strong[Key characteristics:] - Self-describing with embedded metadata -
Follows CF (Climate and Forecast) conventions - Supports compression and
chunking - Ideal for multidimensional array data

=== GRIB (GRIdded Binary)
<grib-gridded-binary>
GRIB (usually .grib or .grb) is the standard format from the World
Meteorological Organization (WMO).

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 3,
    align: (auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([Feature], [GRIB1], [GRIB2],),
    table.hline(),
    [#strong[Introduced];], [1985], [2003],
    [#strong[Template-based];], [Limited], [Extensive],
    [#strong[Compression];], [Simple packing], [Complex packing, JPEG,
    PNG],
    [#strong[Support];], [Legacy], [Modern],
    [#strong[Sections];], [3], [7],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

=== HDF5 (Hierarchical Data Format)
<hdf5-hierarchical-data-format>
HDF5 is a versatile format used in many scientific domains:

- Supports large, complex data
- Excellent compression capabilities
- Used by NASA for Earth observation data
- Can store multiple datasets in one file

=== GeoTIFF
<geotiff>
GeoTIFF embeds georeferencing information into standard TIFF images:

- Common for remote sensing and satellite data
- Supports multiple bands
- Widely supported in GIS software

== Climate Models
<climate-models>
Climate models simulate the Earth's climate system and produce vast
amounts of data.

=== CMIP5 and CMIP6
<cmip5-and-cmip6>
The Coupled Model Intercomparison Project (CMIP) coordinates climate
model experiments worldwide.

`{important} CMIP data is foundational for IPCC assessment reports.`

#strong[CMIP5] (2013): - 50+ modeling groups - \~60 models - RCP
(Representative Concentration Pathway) scenarios - Used in IPCC AR5

#strong[CMIP6] (2019+): - 100+ modeling groups - \~100 models - SSP
(Shared Socioeconomic Pathway) scenarios - Used in IPCC AR6 - Higher
resolution than CMIP5

=== Regional Climate Models (RCMs)
<regional-climate-models-rcms>
Regional models provide higher resolution over specific areas:

- #strong[CORDEX] - Coordinated Regional Climate Downscaling Experiment
- #strong[RegCM] - Regional Climate Model
- #strong[WRF] - Weather Research and Forecasting model
- #strong[RACMO] - Regional Atmospheric Climate Model

`{tip} RCMs typically run at 12-50 km resolution, compared to 100-300 km for global models.`

== Reanalysis Data
<reanalysis-data>
Reanalysis combines observations with model data to create consistent,
long-term climate records.

=== ERA5
<era5>
The European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts (ECMWF) latest
reanalysis:

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 2,
    align: (auto,auto,),
    table.header([Attribute], [Value],),
    table.hline(),
    [#strong[Coverage];], [1940-present],
    [#strong[Resolution];], [0.25° (\~31 km)],
    [#strong[Hourly];], [Yes],
    [#strong[Levels];], [137 hybrid sigma-pressure],
    [#strong[Variables];], [240+],
    [#strong[Size];], [\~2 PB total],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

=== ERA-Interim
<era-interim>
ERA-Interim is ECMWF's earlier reanalysis (1979-2019):

- 0.75° resolution (\~79 km)
- 6-hourly output
- 60 vertical levels
- Being superseded by ERA5

=== MERRA-2
<merra-2>
NASA's Modern-Era Retrospective analysis for Research and Applications,
Version 2:

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 2,
    align: (auto,auto,),
    table.header([Attribute], [Value],),
    table.hline(),
    [#strong[Coverage];], [1980-present],
    [#strong[Resolution];], [0.5° x 0.625°],
    [#strong[Hourly];], [Yes],
    [#strong[Focus];], [Atmospheric, hydrologic cycle],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

=== JRA-55
<jra-55>
Japanese 55-year Reanalysis by JMA:

- 1958-present
- 0.5625° resolution
- 3-hourly output
- 60 levels

== Weather Datasets
<weather-datasets>
=== GFS (Global Forecast System)
<gfs-global-forecast-system>
The US National Weather Service's global model:

- 0.25° resolution
- 16-day forecast
- 4 runs per day (00, 06, 12, 18 UTC)
- Free and open access

=== ICON
<icon>
The ICOsahedral Non-hydrostatic model from DWD (Germany):

- Uses an icosahedral grid (no poles)
- Global and regional versions
- 13 km global resolution
- 7-day forecasts

=== HRRR (High-Resolution Rapid Refresh)
<hrrr-high-resolution-rapid-refresh>
NOAA's convection-allowing model:

- 3 km resolution
- Hourly updates
- 18-hour forecast
- US domain only

== Earth Observation Datasets
<earth-observation-datasets>
Satellite data provides crucial observations of the Earth system:

`{note} CDO can process satellite-derived datasets that follow CF conventions, particularly in NetCDF format.`

