== Hands-On Exercises
<hands-on-exercises>
=== Exercise 1: Install CDO
<exercise-1-install-cdo>
\`\`\`{exercise} :label: ex-introduction-1 Install CDO on your system
using one of the following methods:

+ #strong[Linux (Ubuntu/Debian):]

  ```bash
  sudo apt-get update
  sudo apt-get install cdo
  ```

+ #strong[macOS:]

  ```bash
  brew install cdo
  ```

+ #strong[Windows (WSL):]

  ```bash
  # Install WSL first, then use Ubuntu commands
  sudo apt-get update
  sudo apt-get install cdo
  ```

+ #strong[Conda (any platform):]

  ```bash
  conda install -c conda-forge cdo
  ```

=== Exercise 2: Verify Installation and Explore Help
<exercise-2-verify-installation-and-explore-help>
\`\`\`\`{exercise} :label: ex-introduction-2 Once CDO is installed, run
the following commands and note the output:

```bash
cdo --version
cdo --help | head -50
```

Answer these questions: 1. What version of CDO is installed? 2. How many
operators are listed in the help output? 3. What is the generic CDO
command syntax shown in the help?

`````

### Exercise 3: Explore Available Operators

````{exercise}
:label: ex-introduction-3
Explore CDO's operator categories:

```bash
# Count total operators
cdo --help | grep -c "^ "

# List file operation operators
cdo --help | grep -i "file"

# Look for temporal operators
cdo --help | grep -i "tim"

# Find statistical operators
cdo --help | grep -E "mean|std|min|max" | head -20
```

Create a list of 10 operators that seem useful for your research.
`````

=== Exercise 4: Check Format Support
<exercise-4-check-format-support>
\`\`\`\`{exercise} :label: ex-introduction-4 Check which file formats
your CDO installation supports:

```bash
cdo --help | grep -i "format"
cdo --help | grep -i "netcdf\|grib\|hdf\|tiff"
```

What formats are available? Which format is recommended for general use?
\`\`\`\`

== Quiz
<quiz>
